ctorization can be used for example for\n 899 |     dimensionality reduction, source separation or topic extraction.\n 900 | \n 901 |     The objective function is:\n 902 | \n 903 |         .. math::\n 904 | \n 905 |             0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n 906 | \n 907 |             + alpha\\\\_W * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n 908 | \n 909 |             + alpha\\\\_H * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n 910 | \n 911 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n 912 | \n 913 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n 914 | \n 915 |     Where:\n 916 | \n 917 |     :math:`||A||_{Fro}^2 = \\\\sum_{i,j} A_{ij}^2` (Frobenius norm)\n 918 | \n 919 |     :math:`||vec(A)||_1 = \\\\sum_{i,j} abs(A_{ij})` (Elementwise L1 norm)\n 920 | \n 921 |     The generic norm :math:`||X - WH||_{loss}^2` may represent\n 922 |     the Frobenius norm or another supported beta-divergence loss.\n 923 |     The choice between options is controlled by the `beta_loss` parameter.\n 924 | \n 925 |     The regularization terms are scaled by `n_features` for `W` and by `n_samples` for\n 926 |     `H` to keep their impact balanced with respect to one another and to the data fit\n 927 |     term as independent as possible of the size `n_samples` of the training set.\n 928 | \n 929 |     The objective function is minimized with an alternating minimization of W\n 930 |     and H. If H is given and update_H=False, it solves for W only.\n 931 | \n 932 |     Note that the transformed data is named W and the components matrix is named H. In\n 933 |     the NMF literature, the naming convention is usually the opposite since the data\n 934 |     matrix X is transposed.\n 935 | \n 936 |     Parameters\n 937 |     ----------\n 938 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 939 |         Constant matrix.\n 940 | \n 941 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components), default=None\n 942 |         If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n 943 | \n 944 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features), default=None\n 945 |         If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n 946 |         If update_H=False, it is used as a constant, to solve for W only.\n 947 | \n 948 |     n_components : int, default=None\n 949 |         Number of components, if n_components is not set all features\n 950 |         are kept.\n 951 | \n 952 |     init : {'random', 'nndsvd', 'nndsvda', 'nndsvdar', 'custom'}, default=None\n 953 |         Method used to initialize the procedure.\n 954 | \n 955 |         Valid options:\n 956 | \n 957 |         - None: 'nndsvda' if n_components < n_features, otherwise 'random'.\n 958 | \n 959 |         - 'random': non-negative random matrices, scaled with:\n 960 |             sqrt(X.mean() / n_components)\n 961 | \n 962 |         - 'nndsvd': Nonnegative Double Singular Value Decomposition (NNDSVD)\n 963 |             initialization (better for sparseness)\n 964 | \n 965 |         - 'nndsvda': NNDSVD with zeros filled with the average of X\n 966 |             (better when sparsity is not desired)\n 967 | \n 968 |         - 'nndsvdar': NNDSVD with zeros filled with small random values\n 969 |             (generally faster, less accurate alternative to NNDSVDa\n 970 |             for when sparsity is not desired)\n 971 | \n 972 |         - 'custom': use custom matrices W and H if `update_H=True`. If\n 973 |           `update_H=False`, then only custom matrix H is used.\n 974 | \n 975 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.23\n 976 |             The default value of `init` changed from 'random' to None in 0.23.\n 977 | \n 978 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.1\n 979 |             When `init=None` and n_components is less than n_samples and n_features\n 980 |             defaults to `nndsvda` instead of `nndsvd`.\n 981 | \n 982 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n 983 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n 984 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n 985 | \n 986 |     solver : {'cd', 'mu'}, default='cd'\n 987 |         Numerical solver to use:\n 988 | \n 989 |         - 'cd' is a Coordinate Descent solver that uses Fast Hierarchical\n 990 |             Alternating Least Squares (Fast HALS).\n 991 | \n 992 |         - 'mu' is a Multiplicative Update solver.\n 993 | \n 994 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n 995 |            Coordinate Descent solver.\n 996 | \n 997 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n 998 |            Multiplicative Update solver.\n 999 | \n1000 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n1001 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n1002 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n1003 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n1004 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n1005 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n1006 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros. Used only in 'mu' solver.\n1007 | \n1008 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n1009 | \n1010 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n1011 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n1012 | \n1013 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n1014 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n1015 | \n1016 |     alpha : float, default=0.0\n1017 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms. Set it to zero to have no\n1018 |         regularization. When using `alpha` instead of `alpha_W` and `alpha_H`, the\n1019 |         regularization terms are not scaled by the `n_features` (resp. `n_samples`)\n1020 |         factors for `W` (resp. `H`).\n1021 | \n1022 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1023 |             The `alpha` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in 1.2.\n1024 |             Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1025 | \n1026 |     alpha_W : float, default=0.0\n1027 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `W`. Set it to zero\n1028 |         (default) to have no regularization on `W`.\n1029 | \n1030 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1031 | \n1032 |     alpha_H : float or \"same\", default=\"same\"\n1033 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `H`. Set it to zero to\n1034 |         have no regularization on `H`. If \"same\" (default), it takes the same value as\n1035 |         `alpha_W`.\n1036 | \n1037 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1038 | \n1039 |     l1_ratio : float, default=0.0\n1040 |         The regularization mixing parameter, with 0 <= l1_ratio <= 1.\n1041 |         For l1_ratio = 0 the penalty is an elementwise L2 penalty\n1042 |         (aka Frobenius Norm).\n1043 |         For l1_ratio = 1 it is an elementwise L1 penalty.\n1044 |         For 0 < l1_ratio < 1, the penalty is a combination of L1 and L2.\n1045 | \n1046 |     regularization : {'both', 'components', 'transformation'}, default=None\n1047 |         Select whether the regularization affects the components (H), the\n1048 |         transformation (W), both or none of them.\n1049 | \n1050 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1051 |             The `regularization` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in\n1052 |             1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1053 | \n1054 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1055 |         Used for NMF initialisation (when ``init`` == 'nndsvdar' or\n1056 |         'random'), and in Coordinate Descent. Pass an int for reproducible\n1057 |         results across multiple function calls.\n1058 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1059 | \n1060 |     verbose : int, default=0\n1061 |         The verbosity level.\n1062 | \n1063 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n1064 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n1065 | \n1066 |     Returns\n1067 |     -------\n1068 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1069 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1070 | \n1071 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1072 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1073 | \n1074 |     n_iter : int\n1075 |         Actual number of iterations.\n1076 | \n1077 |     Examples\n1078 |     --------\n1079 |     >>> import numpy as np\n1080 |     >>> X = np.array([[1,1], [2, 1], [3, 1.2], [4, 1], [5, 0.8], [6, 1]])\n1081 |     >>> from sklearn.decomposition import non_negative_factorization\n1082 |     >>> W, H, n_iter = non_negative_factorization(X, n_components=2,\n1083 |     ... init='random', random_state=0)\n1084 | \n1085 |     References\n1086 |     ----------\n1087 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n1088 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n1089 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n1090 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n1091 | \n1092 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n1093 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n1094 |     \"\"\"\n1095 |     X = check_array(X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32])\n1096 | \n1097 |     est = NMF(\n1098 |         n_components=n_components,\n1099 |         init=init,\n1100 |         solver=solver,\n1101 |         beta_loss=beta_loss,\n1102 |         tol=tol,\n1103 |         max_iter=max_iter,\n1104 |         random_state=random_state,\n1105 |         alpha=alpha,\n1106 |         alpha_W=alpha_W,\n1107 |         alpha_H=alpha_H,\n1108 |         l1_ratio=l1_ratio,\n1109 |         verbose=verbose,\n1110 |         shuffle=shuffle,\n1111 |         regularization=regularization,\n1112 |     )\n1113 | \n1114 |     with config_context(assume_finite=True):\n1115 |         W, H, n_iter = est._fit_transform(X, W=W, H=H, update_H=update_H)\n1116 | \n1117 |     return W, H, n_iter\n```\n\nFound class `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass PLSRegression(_PLS):\n\n    # This implementation provides the same results that 3 PLS packages\n    # provided in the R language (R-project):\n    #     - \"mixOmics\" with function pls(X, Y, mode = \"regression\")\n    #     - \"plspm \" with function plsreg2(X, Y)\n    #     - \"pls\" with function oscorespls.fit(X, Y)\n\n    def __init__(\n        self, n_components=2, *, scale=True, max_iter=500, tol=1e-06, copy=True\n    ):\n...\n    def fit(self, X, Y):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound class `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass PLSCanonical(_PLS):\n\n    # This implementation provides the same results that the \"plspm\" package\n    # provided in the R language (R-project), using the function plsca(X, Y).\n    # Results are equal or collinear with the function\n    # ``pls(..., mode = \"canonical\")`` of the \"mixOmics\" package. The\n    # difference relies in the fact that mixOmics implementation does not\n    # exactly implement the Wold algorithm since it does not normalize\n    # y_weights to one.\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        n_components=2,\n        *,\n        scale=True,\n        algorithm=\"nipals\",\n        max_iter=500,\n        tol=1e-06,\n        copy=True,\n    ):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py:check_fit_non_negative`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n3517 | def check_fit_non_ne