h Result:\nFound code snippet in file `examples/cluster/plot_dict_face_patches.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | Online learning of a dictionary of parts of faces\n 3 | =================================================\n 4 | \n 5 | This example uses a large dataset of faces to learn a set of 20 x 20\n 6 | images patches that constitute faces.\n 7 | \n 8 | From the programming standpoint, it is interesting because it shows how\n 9 | to use the online API of the scikit-learn to process a very large\n10 | dataset by chunks. The way we proceed is that we load an image at a time\n11 | and extract randomly 50 patches from this image. Once we have accumulated\n12 | 500 of these patches (using 10 images), we run the\n13 | :func:`~sklearn.cluster.MiniBatchKMeans.partial_fit` method\n14 | of the online KMeans object, MiniBatchKMeans.\n15 | \n16 | The verbose setting on the MiniBatchKMeans enables us to see that some\n17 | clusters are reassigned during the successive calls to\n18 | partial-fit. This is because the number of patches that they represent\n19 | has become too low, and it is better to choose a random new\n20 | cluster.\n21 | \n22 | \"\"\"\n23 | \n24 | # %%\n25 | # Load the data\n26 | # -------------\n27 | \n28 | from sklearn import datasets\n29 | \n30 | faces = datasets.fetch_olivetti_faces()\n31 | \n32 | # %%\n33 | # Learn the dictionary of images\n34 | # ------------------------------\n35 | \n36 | import time\n37 | \n38 | import numpy as np\n39 | \n40 | from sklearn.cluster import MiniBatchKMeans\n41 | from sklearn.feature_extraction.image import extract_patches_2d\n42 | \n43 | print(\"Learning the dictionary... \")\n44 | rng = np.random.RandomState(0)\n45 | kmeans = MiniBatchKMeans(n_clusters=81, random_state=rng, verbose=True)\n46 | patch_size = (20, 20)\n47 | \n48 | buffer = []\n49 | t0 = time.time()\n50 | \n51 | # The online learning part: cycle over the whole dataset 6 times\n52 | index = 0\n53 | for _ in range(6):\n54 |     for img in faces.images:\n55 |         data = extract_patches_2d(img, patch_size, max_patches=50, random_state=rng)\n56 |         data = np.reshape(data, (len(data), -1))\n57 |         buffer.append(data)\n58 |         index += 1\n59 |         if index % 10 == 0:\n60 |             data = np.concatenate(buffer, axis=0)\n61 |             data -= np.mean(data, axis=0)\n62 |             data /= np.std(data, axis=0)\n63 |             kmeans.partial_fit(data)\n64 |             buffer = []\n65 |         if index % 100 == 0:\n66 |             print(\"Partial fit of %4i out of %i\" % (index, 6 * len(faces.images)))\n67 | \n68 | dt = time.time() - t0\n69 | print(\"done in %.2fs.\" % dt)\n70 | \n71 | # %%\n72 | # Plot the results\n73 | # ----------------\n74 | \n75 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/decomposition/_dict_learning.py:dict_learning_online`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 738 | def dict_learning_online(\n 739 |     X,\n 740 |     n_components=2,\n 741 |     *,\n 742 |     alpha=1,\n 743 |     n_iter=\"deprecated\",\n 744 |     max_iter=None,\n 745 |     return_code=True,\n 746 |     dict_init=None,\n 747 |     callback=None,\n 748 |     batch_size=\"warn\",\n 749 |     verbose=False,\n 750 |     shuffle=True,\n 751 |     n_jobs=None,\n 752 |     method=\"lars\",\n 753 |     iter_offset=\"deprecated\",\n 754 |     random_state=None,\n 755 |     return_inner_stats=\"deprecated\",\n 756 |     inner_stats=\"deprecated\",\n 757 |     return_n_iter=\"deprecated\",\n 758 |     positive_dict=False,\n 759 |     positive_code=False,\n 760 |     method_max_iter=1000,\n 761 |     tol=1e-3,\n 762 |     max_no_improvement=10,\n 763 | ):\n 764 |     \"\"\"Solves a dictionary learning matrix factorization problem online.\n 765 | \n 766 |     Finds the best dictionary and the corresponding sparse code for\n 767 |     approximating the data matrix X by solving::\n 768 | \n 769 |         (U^*, V^*) = argmin 0.5 || X - U V ||_Fro^2 + alpha * || U ||_1,1\n 770 |                      (U,V)\n 771 |                      with || V_k ||_2 = 1 for all  0 <= k < n_components\n 772 | \n 773 |     where V is the dictionary and U is the sparse code. ||.||_Fro stands for\n 774 |     the Frobenius norm and ||.||_1,1 stands for the entry-wise matrix norm\n 775 |     which is the sum of the absolute values of all the entries in the matrix.\n 776 |     This is accomplished by repeatedly iterating over mini-batches by slicing\n 777 |     the input data.\n 778 | \n 779 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <DictionaryLearning>`.\n 780 | \n 781 |     Parameters\n 782 |     ----------\n 783 |     X : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 784 |         Data matrix.\n 785 | \n 786 |     n_components : int or None, default=2\n 787 |         Number of dictionary atoms to extract. If None, then ``n_components``\n 788 |         is set to ``n_features``.\n 789 | \n 790 |     alpha : float, default=1\n 791 |         Sparsity controlling parameter.\n 792 | \n 793 |     n_iter : int, default=100\n 794 |         Number of mini-batch iterations to perform.\n 795 | \n 796 |         .. deprecated:: 1.1\n 797 |            `n_iter` is deprecated in 1.1 and will be removed in 1.3. Use\n 798 |            `max_iter` instead.\n 799 | \n 800 |     max_iter : int, default=None\n 801 |         Maximum number of iterations over the complete dataset before\n 802 |         stopping independently of any early stopping criterion heuristics.\n 803 |         If ``max_iter`` is not None, ``n_iter`` is ignored.\n 804 | \n 805 |         .. versionadded:: 1.1\n 806 | \n 807 |     return_code : bool, default=True\n 808 |         Whether to also return the code U or just the dictionary `V`.\n 809 | \n 810 |     dict_init : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features), default=None\n 811 |         Initial value for the dictionary for warm restart scenarios.\n 812 | \n 813 |     callback : callable, default=None