\"Validate types and values of given parameters.\n 30 | \n 31 |     Parameters\n 32 |     ----------\n 33 |     parameter_constraints : dict or {\"no_validation\"}\n 34 |         If \"no_validation\", validation is skipped for this parameter.\n 35 | \n 36 |         If a dict, it must be a dictionary `param_name: list of constraints`.\n 37 |         A parameter is valid if it satisfies one of the constraints from the list.\n 38 |         Constraints can be:\n 39 |         - an Interval object, representing a continuous or discrete range of numbers\n 40 |         - the string \"array-like\"\n 41 |         - the string \"sparse matrix\"\n 42 |         - the string \"random_state\"\n 43 |         - callable\n 44 |         - None, meaning that None is a valid value for the parameter\n 45 |         - any type, meaning that any instance of this type is valid\n 46 |         - an Options object, representing a set of elements of a given type\n 47 |         - a StrOptions object, representing a set of strings\n 48 |         - the string \"boolean\"\n 49 |         - the string \"verbose\"\n 50 |         - the string \"cv_object\"\n 51 |         - the string \"nan\"\n 52 |         - a MissingValues object representing markers for missing values\n 53 |         - a HasMethods object, representing method(s) an object must have\n 54 |         - a Hidden object, representing a constraint not meant to be exposed to the user\n 55 | \n 56 |     params : dict\n 57 |         A dictionary `param_name: param_value`. The parameters to validate against the\n 58 |         constraints.\n 59 | \n 60 |     caller_name : str\n 61 |         The name of the estimator or function or method that called this function.\n 62 |     \"\"\"\n 63 |     for param_name, param_val in params.items():\n 64 |         # We allow parameters to not have a constraint so that third party estimators\n 65 |         # can inherit from sklearn estimators without having to necessarily use the\n 66 |         # validation tools.\n 67 |         if param_name not in parameter_constraints:\n 68 |             continue\n 69 | \n 70 |         constraints = parameter_constraints[param_name]\n 71 | \n 72 |         if constraints == \"no_validation\":\n 73 |             continue\n 74 | \n 75 |         constraints = [make_constraint(constraint) for constraint in constraints]\n 76 | \n 77 |         for constraint in constraints:\n 78 |             if constraint.is_satisfied_by(param_val):\n 79 |                 # this constraint is satisfied, no need to check further.\n 80 |                 break\n 81 |         else:\n 82 |             # No constraint is satisfied, raise with an informative message.\n 83 | \n 84 |             # Ignore constraints that we don't want to expose in the error message,\n 85 |             # i.e. options that are for internal purpose or not officially supported.\n 86 |             constraints = [\n 87 |                 constraint for constraint in constraints if not constraint.hidden\n 88 |             ]\n 89 | \n 90 |             if len(constraints) == 1:\n 91 |                 constraints_str = f\"{constraints[0]}\"\n 92 |             else:\n 93 |                 constraints_str = (\n 94 |                     f\"{', '.join([str(c) for c in constraints[:-1]])} or\"\n 95 |                     f\" {constraints[-1]}\"\n 96 |                 )\n 97 | \n 98 |             raise InvalidParameterError(\n 99 |                 f\"The {param_name!r} parameter of {caller_name} must be\"\n100 |                 f\" {constraints_str}. Got {param_val!r} instead.\"\n101 |             )\n102 | \n103 | \n104 | def make_constraint(constraint):\n105 |     \"\"\"Convert the constraint into the appropriate Constraint object.\n106 | \n107 |     Parameters\n108 |     ----------\n109 |     constraint : object\n110 |         The constraint to convert.\n111 | \n112 |     Returns\n113 |     -------\n114 |     constraint : instance of _Constraint\n115 |         The converted constraint.\n116 |     \"\"\"\n117 |     if isinstance(constraint, str) and constraint == \"array-like\":\n118 |         return _ArrayLikes()\n119 |     if isinstance(constraint, str) and constraint == \"sparse matrix\":\n120 |         return _SparseMatrices()\n121 |     if isinstance(constraint, str) and constraint == \"random_state\":\n122 |         return _RandomStates()\n123 |     if constraint is callable:\n124 |         return _Callables()\n125 |     if constraint is None:\n126 |         return _NoneConstraint()\n127 |     if isinstance(constraint, type):\n128 |         return _InstancesOf(constraint)\n129 |     if isinstance(\n130 |         constraint, (Interval, StrOptions, Options, HasMethods, MissingValues)\n131 |     ):\n132 |         return constraint\n133 |     if isinstance(constraint, str) and constraint == \"boolean\":\n134 |         return _Booleans()\n135 |     if isinstance(constraint, str) and constraint == \"verbose\":\n136 |         return _VerboseHelper()\n137 |     if isinstance(constraint, str) and constraint == \"cv_object\":\n138 |         return _CVObjects()\n139 |     if isinstance(constraint, Hidden):\n140 |         constraint = make_constraint(constraint.constraint)\n141 |         constraint.hidden = True\n142 |         return constraint\n143 |     if isinstance(constraint, str) and constraint == \"nan\":\n144 |         return _NanConstraint()\n145 |     raise ValueError(f\"Unknown constraint type: {constraint}\")\n146 | \n147 | \n148 | def validate_params(parameter_constraints, *, prefer_skip_nested_validation):\n149 |     \"\"\"Decorator to validate types and values of functions and methods.\n150 | \n151 |     Parameters\n152 |     ----------\n153 |     parameter_constraints : dict\n154 |         A dictionary `param_name: list of constraints`. See the docstring of\n155 |         `validate_parameter_constraints` for a description of the accepted constraints.\n156 | \n157 |         Note that the *args and **kwargs parameters are not validated and must not be\n158 |         present in the parameter_constraints dictionary.\n159 | \n160 |     prefer_skip_nested_validation : bool\n161 |         If True, the validation of parameters of inner estimators or functions\n162 |         called by the decorated function will be skipped.\n163 | \n164 |         This is useful to avoid validating many times the parameters passed by the\n165 |         user from the public facing API. It's also useful to avoid validating\n166 |         parameters that we pass internally to inner functions that are guaranteed to\n167 |         be valid by the test suite.\n168 | \n169 |         It should be set to True for most functions, except for those that receive\n170 |         non-validated objects as parameters or that are just wrappers around classes\n171 |         because they only perform a partial validation.\n172 | \n173 |     Returns\n174 |     -------\n175 |     decorated_function : function or method\n176 |         The decorated function.\n177 |     \"\"\"\n178 | \n179 |     def decorator(func):\n180 |         # The dict of parameter constraints is set as an attribute of the function\n181 |         # to make it possible to dynamically introspect the constraints for\n182 |         # automatic testing.\n183 |         setattr(func, \"_skl_parameter_constraints\", parameter_constraints)\n184 | \n185 |         @functools.wraps(func)\n186 |         def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n187 |             global_skip_validation = get_config()[\"skip_parameter_validation\"]\n188 |             if global_skip_validation:\n189 |                 return func(*args, **kwargs)\n190 | \n191 |             func_sig = signature(func)\n192 | \n193 |             # Map *args/**kwargs to the function signature\n194 |             params = func_sig.bind(*args, **kwargs)\n195 |             params.apply_defaults()\n196 | \n197 |             # ignore self/cls and positional/keyword markers\n198 |             to_ignore = [\n199 |                 p.name\n200 |                 for p in func_sig.parameters.values()\n201 |                 if p.kind in (p.VAR_POSITIONAL, p.VAR_KEYWORD)\n202 |             ]\n203 |             to_ignore += [\"self\", \"cls\"]\n204 |             params = {k: v for k, v in params.arguments.items() if k not in to_ignore}\n205 | \n206 |             validate_parameter_constraints(\n207 |                 parameter_constraints, params, caller_name=func.__qualname__\n208 |             )\n209 | \n210 |             try:\n211 |                 with config_context(\n212 |                     skip_parameter_validation=(\n213 |                         prefer_skip_nested_validation or global_skip_validation\n214 |                     )\n215 |                 ):\n216 |                     return func(*args, **kwargs)\n217 |             except InvalidParameterError as e:\n218 |                 # When the function is just a wrapper around an estimator, we allow\n219 |                 # the function to delegate validation to the estimator, but we replace\n220 |                 # the name of the estimator by the name of the function in the error\n221 |                 # message to avoid confusion.\n222 |                 msg = re.sub(\n223 |                     r\"parameter of \\w+ must be\",\n224 |                     f\"parameter of {func.__qualname__} must be\",\n225 |                     str(e),\n226 |                 )\n227 |                 raise InvalidParameterError(msg) from e\n228 | \n229 |         return wrapper\n230 | \n231 |     return decorator\n232 | \n233 | \n234 | class RealNotInt(Real):\n235 |     \"\"\"A type that represents reals that are not instances of int.\n236 | \n237 |     Behaves like float, but also works with values extracted from numpy arrays.\n238 |     isintance(1, RealNotInt) -> False\n239 |     isinstance(1.0, RealNotInt) -> True\n240 |     \"\"\"\n241 | \n242 | \n243 | RealNotInt.register(float)\n244 | \n245 | \n246 | def _type_name(t):\n247 |     \"\"\"Convert type into human readable string.\"\"\"\n248 |     module = t.__module__\n249 |     qualname = t.__qualname__\n250 |     if module == \"builtins\":\n251 |         return qualname\n252 |     elif t == Real:\n253 |         return \"float\"\n254 |     elif t == Integral:\n255 |         return \"int\"\n256 |     return f\"{module}.{qualname}\"\n257 | \n258 | \n259 | class _Constraint(ABC):\n260 |     \"\"\"Base class for the constraint objects.\"\"\"\n261 | \n262 |     def __init__(self):\n263 |         self.hidden = False\n264 | \n265 |     @abstractmethod\n266 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n267 |         \"\"\"Whether or not a value satisfies the constraint.\n268 | \n269 |         Parameters\n270 |         ----------\n271 |         val : object\n272 |             The value to check.\n273 | \n274 |         Returns\n275 |         -------\n276 |         is_satisfied : bool\n277 |             Whether or not the constraint is satisfied by this value.\n278 |         \"\"\"\n279 | \n280 |     @abstractmethod\n281 |     def __str__(self):\n282 |         \"\"\"A human readable representational string of the constraint.\"\"\"\n283 | \n284 | \n285 | class _InstancesOf(_Constraint):\n286 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing instances of a given type.\n287 | \n288 |     Parameters\n289 |     ----------\n290 |     type : type\n291 |         The valid type.\n292 |     \"\"\"\n293 | \n294 |     def __init__(self, type):\n295 |         super().__init__()\n296 |         self.type = type\n297 | \n298 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n299 |         return isinstance(val, self.type)\n300 | \n301 |     def __str__(self):\n302 |         return f\"an instance of {_type_name(self.type)!r}\"\n303 | \n304 | \n305 | class _NoneConstraint(_Constraint):\n306 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing the None singleton.\"\"\"\n307 | \n308 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n309 |         return val is None\n310 | \n311 |     def __str__(self):\n312 |         return \"None\"\n313 | \n314 | \n315 | class _NanConstraint(_Constraint):\n316 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing the indicator `np.nan`.\"\"\"\n317 | \n318 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n319 |         return (\n320 |             not isinstance(val, Integral) and isinstance(val, Real) and math.isnan(val)\n321 |         )\n322 | \n323 |     def __str__(self):\n324 |         return \"numpy.nan\"\n325 | \n326 | \n327 | class _PandasNAConstraint(_Constraint):\n328 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing the indicator `pd.NA`.\"\"\"\n329 | \n330 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n331 |         try:\n332 |             import pandas as pd\n333 | \n334 |             return isinstance(val, type(pd.NA)) and pd.isna(val)\n335 |         except ImportError:\n336 |             return False\n337 | \n338 |     def __str__(self):\n339 |         return \"pandas.NA\"\n340 | \n341 | \n342 | class Options(_Constraint):\n343 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing a finite set of instances of a given type.\n344 | \n345 |     Parameters\n346 |     ----------\n347 |     type : type\n348 | \n349 |     options : set\n350 |         The set of valid scalars.\n351 | \n352 |     deprecated : set or None, default=None\n353 |         A subset of the `options` to mark as deprecated in the string\n354 |         representation of the constraint.\n355 |     \"\"\"\n356 | \n357 |     def __init__(self, type, options, *, deprecated=None):\n358 |         super().__init__()\n359 |         self.type = type\n360 |         self.options = options\n361 |         self.deprecated = deprecated or set()\n362 | \n363 |         if self.deprecated - self.options:\n364 |             raise ValueError(\"The deprecated options must be a subset of the options.\")\n365 | \n366 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n367 |         return isinstance(val, self.type) and val in self.options\n368 | \n369 |     def _mark_if_deprecated(self, option):\n370 |         \"\"\"Add a deprecated mark to an option if needed.\"\"\"\n371 |         option_str = f\"{option!r}\"\n372 |         if option in self.deprecated:\n373 |             option_str = f\"{option_str} (deprecated)\"\n374 |         return option_str\n375 | \n376 |     def __str__(self):\n377 |         options_str = (\n378 |             f\"{', '.join([self._mark_if_deprecated(o) for o in self.options])}\"\n379 |         )\n380 |         return f\"a {_type_name(self.type)} among {{{options_str}}}\"\n381 | \n382 | \n383 | class StrOptions(Options):\n384 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing a finite set of strings.\n385 | \n386 |     Parameters\n387 |     ----------\n388 |     options : set of str\n389 |         The set of valid strings.\n390 | \n391 |     deprecated : set of str or None, default=None\n392 |         A subset of the `options` to mark as deprecated in the string\n393 |         representation of the constraint.\n394 |     \"\"\"\n395 | \n396 |     def __init__(self, options, *, deprecated=None):\n397 |         super().__init__(type=str, options=options, deprecated=deprecated)\n398 | \n399 | \n400 | class Interval(_Constraint):\n401 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing a typed interval.\n402 | \n403 |     Parameters\n404 |     ----------\n405 |     type : {numbers.Integral, numbers.Real, RealNotInt}\n406 |         The set of numbers in which to set the interval.\n407 | \n408 |         If RealNotInt, only reals that don't have the integer type\n409 |         are allowed. For example 1.0 is allowed but 1 is not.\n410 | \n411 |     left : float or int or None\n412 |         The left bound of the interval. None means left bound is -\u221e.\n413 | \n414 |     right : float, int or None\n415 |         The right bound of the interval. None means right bound is +\u221e.\n416 | \n417 |     closed : {\"left\", \"right\", \"both\", \"neither\"}\n418 |         Whether the interval is open or closed. Possible choices are:\n419 | \n420 |         - `\"left\"`: the interval is closed on the left and open on the right.\n421 |           It is equivalent to the interval `[ left, right )`.\n422 |         - `\"right\"`: the interval is closed on the right and open on the left.\n423 |           It is equivalent to the interval `( left, right ]`.\n424 |         - `\"both\"`: the interval is closed.\n425 |           It is equivalent to the interval `[ left, right ]`.\n426 |         - `\"neither\"`: the interval is open.\n427 |           It is equivalent to the interval `( left, right )`.\n428 | \n429 |     Notes\n430 |     -----\n431 |     Setting a bound to `None` and setting the interval closed is valid. For instance,\n432 |     strictly speaking, `Interval(Real, 0, None, closed=\"both\")` corresponds to\n433 |     `[0, +\u221e) U {+\u221e}`.\n434 |     \"\"\"\n435 | \n436 |     def __init__(self, type, left, right, *, closed):\n437 |         super().__init__()\n438 |         self.type = type\n439 |         self.left = left\n440 |         self.right = right\n441 |         self.closed = closed\n442 | \n443 |         self._check_params()\n444 | \n445 |     def _check_params(self):\n446 |         if self.type not in (Integral, Real, RealNotInt):\n447 |             raise ValueError(\n448 |                 \"type must be either numbers.Integral, numbers.Real or RealNotInt.\"\n449 |                 f\" Got {self.type} instead.\"\n450 |             )\n451 | \n452 |         if self.closed not in (\"left\", \"right\", \"both\", \"neither\"):\n453 |             raise ValueError(\n454 |                 \"closed must be either 'left', 'right', 'both' or 'neither'. \"\n455 |                 f\"Got {self.closed} instead.\"\n456 |             )\n457 | \n458 |         if self.type is Integral:\n459 |             suffix = \"for an interval over the integers.\"\n460 |             if self.left is not None and not isinstance(self.left, Integral):\n461 |                 raise TypeError(f\"Expecting left to be an int {suffix}\")\n462 |             if self.right is not None and not isinstance(self.right, Integral):\n463 |                 raise TypeError(f\"Expecting right to be an int {suffix}\")\n464 |             if self.left is None and self.closed in (\"left\", \"both\"):\n465 |                 raise ValueError(\n466 |                     f\"left can't be None when closed == {self.closed} {suffix}\"\n467 |                 )\n468 |             if self.right is None and self.closed in (\"right\", \"both\"):\n469 |                 raise ValueError(\n470 |                     f\"right can't be None when closed == {self.closed} {suffix}\"\n471 |                 )\n472 |         else:\n473 |             if self.left is not None and not isinstance(self.left, Real):\n474 |                 raise TypeError(\"Expecting left to be a real number.\")\n475 |             if self.right is not None and not isinstance(self.right, Real):\n476 |                 raise TypeError(\"Expecting right to be a real number.\")\n477 | \n478 |         if self.right is not None and self.left is not None and self.right <= self.left:\n479 |             raise ValueError(\n480 |                 f\"right can't be less than left. Got left={self.left} and \"\n481 |                 f\"right={self.right}\"\n482 |             )\n483 | \n484 |     def __contains__(self, val):\n485 |         if not isinstance(val, Integral) and np.isnan(val):\n486 |             return False\n487 | \n488 |         left_cmp = operator.lt if self.closed in (\"left\", \"both\") else operator.le\n489 |         right_cmp = operator.gt if self.closed in (\"right\", \"both\") else operator.ge\n490 | \n491 |         left = -np.inf if self.left is None else self.left\n492 |         right = np.inf if self.right is None else self.right\n493 | \n494 |         if left_cmp(val, left):\n495 |             return False\n496 |         if right_cmp(val, right):\n497 |             return False\n498 |         return True\n499 | \n500 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n501 |         if not isinstance(val, self.type):\n502 |             return False\n503 | \n504 |         return val in self\n505 | \n506 |     def __str__(self):\n507 |         type_str = \"an int\" if self.type is Integral else \"a float\"\n508 |         left_bracket = \"[\" if self.closed in (\"left\", \"both\") else \"(\"\n509 |         left_bound = \"-inf\" if self.left is None else self.left\n510 |         right_bound = \"inf\" if self.right is None else self.right\n511 |         right_bracket = \"]\" if self.closed in (\"right\", \"both\") else \")\"\n512 | \n513 |         # better repr if the bounds were given as integers\n514 |         if not self.type == Integral and isinstance(self.left, Real):\n515 |             left_bound = float(left_bound)\n516 |         if not self.type == Integral and isinstance(self.right, Real):\n517 |             right_bound = float(right_bound)\n518 | \n519 |         return (\n520 |             f\"{type_str} in the range \"\n521 |             f\"{left_bracket}{left_bound}, {right_bound}{right_bracket}\"\n522 |         )\n523 | \n524 | \n525 | class _ArrayLikes(_Constraint):\n526 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing array-likes\"\"\"\n527 | \n528 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n529 |         return _is_arraylike_not_scalar(val)\n530 | \n531 |     def __str__(self):\n532 |         return \"an array-like\"\n533 | \n534 | \n535 | class _SparseMatrices(_Constraint):\n536 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing sparse matrices.\"\"\"\n537 | \n538 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n539 |         return issparse(val)\n540 | \n541 |     def __str__(self):\n542 |         return \"a sparse matrix\"\n543 | \n544 | \n545 | class _Callables(_Constraint):\n546 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing callables.\"\"\"\n547 | \n548 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n549 |         return callable(val)\n550 | \n551 |     def __str__(self):\n552 |         return \"a callable\"\n553 | \n554 | \n555 | class _RandomStates(_Constraint):\n556 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing random states.\n557 | \n558 |     Convenience class for\n559 |     [Interval(Integral, 0, 2**32 - 1, closed=\"both\"), np.random.RandomState, None]\n560 |     \"\"\"\n561 | \n562 |     def __init__(self):\n563 |         super().__init__()\n564 |         self._constraints = [\n565 |             Interval(Integral, 0, 2**32 - 1, closed=\"both\"),\n566 |             _InstancesOf(np.random.RandomState),\n567 |             _NoneConstraint(),\n568 |         ]\n569 | \n570 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n571 |         return any(c.is_satisfied_by(val) for c in self._constraints)\n572 | \n573 |     def __str__(self):\n574 |         return (\n575 |             f\"{', '.join([str(c) for c in self._constraints[:-1]])} or\"\n576 |             f\" {self._constraints[-1]}\"\n577 |         )\n578 | \n579 | \n580 | class _Booleans(_Constraint):\n581 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing boolean likes.\n582 | \n583 |     Convenience class for\n584 |     [bool, np.bool_]\n585 |     \"\"\"\n586 | \n587 |     def __init__(self):\n588 |         super().__init__()\n589 |         self._constraints = [\n590 |             _InstancesOf(bool),\n591 |             _InstancesOf(np.bool_),\n592 |         ]\n593 | \n594 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n595 |         return any(c.is_satisfied_by(val) for c in self._constraints)\n596 | \n597 |     def __str__(self):\n598 |         return (\n599 |             f\"{', '.join([str(c) for c in self._constraints[:-1]])} or\"\n600 |             f\" {self._constraints[-1]}\"\n601 |         )\n602 | \n603 | \n604 | class _VerboseHelper(_Constraint):\n605 |     \"\"\"Helper constraint for the verbose parameter.\n606 | \n607 |     Convenience class for\n608 |     [Interval(Integral, 0, None, closed=\"left\"), bool, numpy.bool_]\n609 |     \"\"\"\n610 | \n611 |     def __init__(self):\n612 |         super().__init__()\n613 |         self._constraints = [\n614 |             Interval(Integral, 0, None, closed=\"left\"),\n615 |             _InstancesOf(bool),\n616 |             _InstancesOf(np.bool_),\n617 |         ]\n618 | \n619 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n620 |         return any(c.is_satisfied_by(val) for c in self._constraints)\n621 | \n622 |     def __str__(self):\n623 |         return (\n624 |             f\"{', '.join([str(c) for c in self._constraints[:-1]])} or\"\n625 |             f\" {self._constraints[-1]}\"\n626 |         )\n627 | \n628 | \n629 | class MissingValues(_Constraint):\n630 |     \"\"\"Helper constraint for the `missing_values` parameters.\n631 | \n632 |     Convenience for\n633 |     [\n634 |         Integral,\n635 |         Interval(Real, None, None, closed=\"both\"),\n636 |         str,   # when numeric_only is False\n637 |         None,  # when numeric_only is False\n638 |         _NanConstraint(),\n639 |         _PandasNAConstraint(),\n640 |     ]\n641 | \n642 |     Parameters\n643 |     ----------\n644 |     numeric_only : bool, default=False\n645 |         Whether to consider only numeric missing value markers.\n646 | \n647 |     \"\"\"\n648 | \n649 |     def __init__(self, numeric_only=False):\n650 |         super().__init__()\n651 | \n652 |         self.numeric_only = numeric_only\n653 | \n654 |         self._constraints = [\n655 |             _InstancesOf(Integral),\n656 |             # we use an interval of Real to ignore np.nan that has its own constraint\n657 |             Interval(Real, None, None, closed=\"both\"),\n658 |             _NanConstraint(),\n659 |             _PandasNAConstraint(),\n660 |         ]\n661 |         if not self.numeric_only:\n662 |             self._constraints.extend([_InstancesOf(str), _NoneConstraint()])\n663 | \n664 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n665 |         return any(c.is_satisfied_by(val) for c in self._constraints)\n666 | \n667 |     def __str__(self):\n668 |         return (\n669 |             f\"{', '.join([str(c) for c in self._constraints[:-1]])} or\"\n670 |             f\" {self._constraints[-1]}\"\n671 |         )\n672 | \n673 | \n674 | class HasMethods(_Constraint):\n675 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing objects that expose specific methods.\n676 | \n677 |     It is useful for parameters following a protocol and where we don't want to impose\n678 |     an affiliation to a specific module or class.\n679 | \n680 |     Parameters\n681 |     ----------\n682 |     methods : str or list of str\n683 |         The method(s) that the object is expected to expose.\n684 |     \"\"\"\n685 | \n686 |     @validate_params(\n687 |         {\"methods\": [str, list]},\n688 |         prefer_skip_nested_validation=True,\n689 |     )\n690 |     def __init__(self, methods):\n691 |         super().__init__()\n692 |         if isinstance(methods, str):\n693 |             methods = [methods]\n694 |         self.methods = methods\n695 | \n696 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n697 |         return all(callable(getattr(val, method, None)) for method in self.methods)\n698 | \n699 |     def __str__(self):\n700 |         if len(self.methods) == 1:\n701 |             methods = f\"{self.methods[0]!r}\"\n702 |         else:\n703 |             methods = (\n704 |                 f\"{', '.join([repr(m) for m in self.methods[:-1]])} and\"\n705 |                 f\" {self.methods[-1]!r}\"\n706 |             )\n707 |         return f\"an object implementing {methods}\"\n708 | \n709 | \n710 | class _IterablesNotString(_Constraint):\n711 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing iterables that are not strings.\"\"\"\n712 | \n713 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n714 |         return isinstance(val, Iterable) and not isinstance(val, str)\n715 | \n716 |     def __str__(self):\n717 |         return \"an iterable\"\n718 | \n719 | \n720 | class _CVObjects(_Constraint):\n721 |     \"\"\"Constraint representing cv objects.\n722 | \n723 |     Convenient class for\n724 |     [\n725 |         Interval(Integral, 2, None, closed=\"left\"),\n726 |         HasMethods([\"split\", \"get_n_splits\"]),\n727 |         _IterablesNotString(),\n728 |         None,\n729 |     ]\n730 |     \"\"\"\n731 | \n732 |     def __init__(self):\n733 |         super().__init__()\n734 |         self._constraints = [\n735 |             Interval(Integral, 2, None, closed=\"left\"),\n736 |             HasMethods([\"split\", \"get_n_splits\"]),\n737 |             _IterablesNotString(),\n738 |             _NoneConstraint(),\n739 |         ]\n740 | \n741 |     def is_satisfied_by(self, val):\n742 |         return any(c.is_satisfied_by(val) for c in self._constraints)\n743 | \n744 |     def __str__(self):\n745 |         return (\n746 |             f\"{', '.join([str(c) for c in self._constraints[:-1]])} or\"\n747 |             f\" {self._constraints[-1]}\"\n748 |         )\n749 | \n750 | \n751 | class Hidden:\n752 |     \"\"\"Class encapsulating a constraint not meant to be exposed to the user.\n753 | \n754 |     Parameters\n755 |     ----------\n756 |     constraint : str or _Constraint instance\n757 |         The constraint to be used internally.\n758 |     \"\"\"\n759 | \n760 |     def __init__(self, constraint):\n761 |         self.constraint = constraint\n762 | \n763 | \n764 | def generate_invalid_param_val(constraint):\n765 |     \"\"\"Return a value that does not satisfy the constraint.\n766 | \n767 |     Raises a NotImplementedError if there exists no invalid value for this constraint.\n768 | \n769 |     This is only useful for testing purpose.\n770 | \n771 |     Parameters\n772 |     ----------\n773 |     constraint : _Constraint instance\n774 |         The constraint to generate a value for.\n775 | \n776 |     Returns\n777 |     -------\n778 |     val : object\n779 |         A value that does not satisfy the constraint.\n780 |     \"\"\"\n781 |     if isinstance(constraint, StrOptions):\n782 |         return f\"not {' or '.join(constraint.options)}\"\n783 | \n784 |     if isinstance(constraint, MissingValues):\n785 |         return np.array([1, 2, 3])\n786 | \n787 |     if isinstance(constraint, _VerboseHelper):\n788 |         return -1\n789 | \n790 |     if isinstance(constraint, HasMethods):\n791 |         return type(\"HasNotMethods\", (), {})()\n792 | \n793 |     if isinstance(constraint, _IterablesNotString):\n794 |         return \"a string\"\n795 | \n796 |     if isinstance(constraint, _CVObjects):\n797 |         return \"not a cv object\"\n798 | \n799 |     if isinstance(constraint, Interval) and constraint.type is Integral:\n800 |         if constraint.left is not None:\n801 |             return constraint.left - 1\n802 |         if constraint.right is not None:\n803 |             return constraint.right + 1\n804 | \n805 |         # There's no integer outside (-inf, +inf)\n806 |         raise NotImplementedError\n807 | \n808 |     if isinstance(constraint, Interval) and constraint.type in (Real, RealNotInt):\n809 |         if constraint.left is not None:\n810 |             return constraint.left - 1e-6\n811 |         if constraint.right is not None:\n812 |             return constraint.right + 1e-6\n813 | \n814 |         # bounds are -inf, +inf\n815 |         if constraint.closed in (\"right\", \"neither\"):\n816 |             return -np.inf\n817 |         if constraint.closed in (\"left\", \"neither\"):\n818 |             return np.inf\n819 | \n820 |         # interval is [-inf, +inf]\n821 |         return np.nan\n822 | \n823 |     raise NotImplementedError\n824 | \n825 | \n826 | def generate_valid_param(constraint):\n827 |     \"\"\"Return a value that does satisfy a constraint.\n828 | \n829 |     This is only useful for testing purpose.\n830 | \n831 |     Parameters\n832 |     ----------\n833 |     constraint : Constraint instance\n834 |         The constraint to generate a value for.\n835 | \n836 |     Returns\n837 |     -------\n838 |     val : object\n839 |         A value that does satisfy the constraint.\n840 |     \"\"\"\n841 |     if isinstance(constraint, _ArrayLikes):\n842 |         return np.array([1, 2, 3])\n843 | \n844 |     if isinstance(constraint, _SparseMatrices):\n845 |         return csr_matrix([[0, 1], [1, 0]])\n846 | \n847 |     if isinstance(constraint, _RandomStates):\n848 |         return np.random.RandomState(42)\n849 | \n850 |     if isinstance(constraint, _Callables):\n851 |         return lambda x: x\n852 | \n853 |     if isinstance(constraint, _NoneConstraint):\n854 |         return None\n855 | \n856 |     if isinstance(constraint, _InstancesOf):\n857 |         if constraint.type is np.ndarray:\n858 |             # special case for ndarray since it can't be instantiated without arguments\n859 |             return np.array([1, 2, 3])\n860 | \n861 |         if constraint.type in (Integral, Real):\n862 |             # special case for Integral and Real since they are abstract classes\n863 |             return 1\n864 | \n865 |         return constraint.type()\n866 | \n867 |     if isinstance(constraint, _Booleans):\n868 |         return True\n869 | \n870 |     if isinstance(constraint, _VerboseHelper):\n871 |         return 1\n872 | \n873 |     if isinstance(constraint, MissingValues) and constraint.numeric_only:\n874 |         return np.nan\n875 | \n876 |     if isinstance(constraint, MissingValues) and not constraint.numeric_only:\n877 |         return \"missing\"\n878 | \n879 |     if isinstance(constraint, HasMethods):\n880 |         return type(\n881 |             \"ValidHasMethods\", (), {m: lambda self: None for m in constraint.methods}\n882 |         )()\n883 | \n884 |     if isinstance(constraint, _IterablesNotString):\n885 |         return [1, 2, 3]\n886 | \n887 |     if isinstance(constraint, _CVObjects):\n888 |         return 5\n889 | \n890 |     if isinstance(constraint, Options):  # includes StrOptions\n891 |         for option in constraint.options:\n892 |             return option\n893 | \n894 |     if isinstance(constraint, Interval):\n895 |         interval = constraint\n896 |         if interval.left is None and interval.right is None:\n897 |             return 0\n898 |         elif interval.left is None:\n899 |             return interval.right - 1\n900 |         elif interval.right is None:\n901 |             return interval.left + 1\n902 |         else:\n903 |             if interval.type is Real:\n904 |                 return (interval.left + interval.right) / 2\n905 |             else:\n906 |                 return interval.left + 1\n907 | \n908 |     raise ValueError(f\"Unknown constraint type: {constraint}\")\n909 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the analysis of the codebase and issue description, I can now provide the locations that are relevant to the implementation of the `assert_consistent_docs()` feature. Here's the output in the requested format:\n\n```\nsklearn/utils/validation.py\nline: 469\nline: 470\nline: 471\nline: 472\nline: 473\nfunction: check_consistent_length\n\nsklearn/utils/_param_validation.py\nline: 28\nline: 29\nline: 30\nclass: Validation\nfunction: validate_parameters\n\nsklearn/base.py\nline: 1472\nline: 1473\nline: 1474\nline: 1475\nline: 1476\nline: 1477\nline: 1478\nline: 1479\nline: 1480\nline: 1481\nline: 1482\nline: 1483\nline: 1484\nline: 1485\nline: 1486\nline: 1487\nline: 1488\nline: 1489\nline: 1490\nline: 1491\nline: 1492\nline: 1493\nline: 1494\nline: 1495\nline: 1496\nline: 1497\nline: 1498\nline: 1499\nline: 1500\nline: 1501\nline: 1502\nline: 1503\nline: 1504\nline: 1505\nline: 1506\nline: 1507\nline: 1508\nline: 1509\nline: 1510\nline: 1511\nline: 1512\nline: 1513\nline: 1514\nline: 1515\nline: 1516\nline: 1517\nfunction: _fit_context\n\nsklearn/utils/deprecation.py\nline: 10\nline: 11\nline: 12\nline: 13\nline: 14\nline: 15\nline: 16\nline: 17\nline: 18\nline: 19\nline: 20\nline: 21\nline: 22\nline: 23\nline: 24\nline: 25\nline: 26\nline: 27\nline: 28\nline: 29\nline: 30\nline: 31\nline: 32\nline: 33\nline: 34\nline: 35\nline: 36\nline: 37\nline: 38\nclass: deprecated\nfunction: __init__\n\nsklearn/utils/_pprint.py\nline: 77\nline: 78\nline: 79\nline: 80\nline: 81\nline: 82\nline: 83\nline: 84\nline: 85\nline: 86\nline: 87\nline: 88\nclass: KeyValTuple\nclass: KeyValTupleParam\n```\n\nThe reason for selecting these locations:\n\n1. The main implementation of `assert_consistent_docs()` would likely go in `sklearn/utils/validation.py` since it deals with docstring consistency validation, which is related to the validation utilities already present in the file.\n\n2. The `check_consistent_length()` function in `sklearn/utils/validation.py` shows the pattern for consistent docstring validation, especially in handling parameters and returns sections.\n\n3. `sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py` contains the base validation framework used throughout scikit-learn, which is relevant for understanding how parameters and return values are documented.\n\n4. `sklearn/base.py:_fit_context` shows how parameters are documented and validated, which is crucial for implementing consistent docstring checking.\n\n5. `sklearn/utils/deprecation.py:deprecated` shows the pattern for documenting deprecated features consistently.\n\n6. `sklearn/utils/_pprint.py` contains utility classes for pretty-printing, which helps understand how parameters and their documentation are structured.\n\nThese locations form a comprehensive set of utility functions and patterns that would be needed to implement the docstring consistency checker according to the issue description.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_artifacts", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"artifact_type": {"type": "string", "enum": ["all", "function", "method", "class"], "default": "all", "description": "Optional exact filter for artifact definition type."}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.", "default": null}, "name_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 8, "description": "Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice."}}, "required": []}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 138109, "completion_tokens": 1251}, "time": 255.0011169910431}]}}
{"instance_id": "pandas-dev__pandas-60457", "found_files": [["pandas/io/formats/csvs.py", "pandas/core/frame.py", "pandas/core/indexes/base.py"]], "found_modules": [["pandas/io/formats/csvs.py:CSVFormatter", "pandas/core/apply.py:FrameColumnApply", "pandas/core/resample.py:Resampler", "pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame", "pandas/core/groupby/generic.py:SeriesGroupBy", "pandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py:NumpyEADtype", "pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:LinePlot", "pandas/core/indexes/base.py:Index"]], "found_entities": [["pandas/io/formats/csvs.py:CSVFormatter.float_format", "pandas/io/formats/csvs.py:CSVFormatter._number_format", "pandas/core/apply.py:FrameColumnApply.infer_to_same_shape", "pandas/core/resample.py:Resampler.sem", "pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame._get_values_for_csv", "pandas/core/groupby/generic.py:SeriesGroupBy.dtype", "pandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py:NumpyEADtype.__repr__", "pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:LinePlot._is_ts_plot", "pandas/core/indexes/base.py:Index._get_values_for_csv"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on my analysis, I can now provide the locations that need modification to improve the documentation for the float_format parameter in to_csv. Here are the relevant locations:\n\n```\npandas/io/formats/csvs.py\nline: 3767-3769\nfunction: NDFrame.to_csv\nclass: CSVFormatter\n\npandas/io/formats/csvs.py\nline: 106-107\nclass: CSVFormatter\nfunction: float_format\n\npandas/io/formats/csvs.py\nline: 180-182\nclass: CSVFormatter\nfunction: _number_format\n\npandas/core/frame.py\nline: 1359-1376\nfunction: DataFrame._get_values_for_csv\n\npandas/core/indexes/base.py\nline: 1521-1537\nfunction: Index._get_values_for_csv\n```\n\nThese locations are ordered by importance, with the primary documentation area being listed first. The implementation shows that:\n\n1. The main documentation needs examples for the float_format parameter in to_csv\n2. The CSVFormatter class handles the float formatting internally\n3. The _number_format property dictionary contains the formatting settings\n4. The actual formatting logic is implemented in _get_values_for_csv\n5. The base Index implementation also uses float formatting\n\nThe most critical files are listed first, as they contain the core functionality and formatting logic that users need to understand when working with float_format. The implementation shows that the parameter can take either a string format or a callable, and the documentation needs to clearly explain both usage patterns with practical examples."], "meta_data": {"repo": "pandas-dev/pandas", "base_commit": "844b3191bd45b95cbaae341048bf7f367f086f2f", "problem_statement": "DOC: Add examples for float_format in to_csv documentation\n### Pandas version checks\r\n\r\n- [X] I have checked that the issue still exists on the latest versions of the docs on `main` [here](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/dev/)\r\n\r\n\r\n### Location of the documentation\r\n\r\nhttps://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.to_csv.html\r\n\r\n### Documentation problem\r\n\r\nThe float_format parameter in to_csv is explained but lacks examples. Users might struggle to understand how to apply this parameter effectively without concrete examples in the documentation.\r\n\r\n### Suggested fix for documentation\r\n\r\nI suggest adding examples for float_format to make the documentation more beginner-friendly. Examples could include:\r\n\r\n```\r\n# Format floats to two decimal places\r\ndf.to_csv(\"example1.csv\", float_format=\"%.2f\")\r\n\r\n# Use scientific notation\r\ndf.to_csv(\"example2.csv\", float_format=\"{:.2e}\".format)\r\n```\n", "patch": "diff --git a/pandas/core/generic.py b/pandas/core/generic.py\nindex a6be17a654aa7..3a48cc8a66076 100644\n--- a/pandas/core/generic.py\n+++ b/pandas/core/generic.py\n@@ -3878,6 +3878,14 @@ def to_csv(\n         >>> import os  # doctest: +SKIP\n         >>> os.makedirs(\"folder/subfolder\", exist_ok=True)  # doctest: +SKIP\n         >>> df.to_csv(\"folder/subfolder/out.csv\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n+\n+        Format floats to two decimal places:\n+\n+        >>> df.to_csv(\"out1.csv\", float_format=\"%.2f\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n+\n+        Format floats using scientific notation:\n+\n+        >>> df.to_csv(\"out2.csv\", float_format=\"{{:.2e}}\".format)  # doctest: +SKIP\n         \"\"\"\n         df = self if isinstance(self, ABCDataFrame) else self.to_frame()\n \n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 86294, "completion_tokens": 568}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nget_trace_artifacts(artifact_type: str = 'all', path_contains: str | None = None, name_contains: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 8):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n    - executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n    - tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n    - then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n    Args:\n    - `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n    - `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n    - `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n    - `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n    - `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n    - `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n    - `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n    - `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n    - Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nget_trace_data_dependencies(symbol_contains: str | None = None, scope_contains: str | None = None, path_contains: str | None = None, edge_type: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 12, path_limit: int = 4):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    - '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' is generated by analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'.\n    - It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts, which can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n    Args:\n    - `symbol_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to symbols with `DataLoader` in their names, you can set `symbol_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    Together with `scope_contains` and `path_contains`, this first selects a set of matching symbols; an edge is retained when either endpoint belongs to that set.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `scope_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.\n    The scope is represented as a dot-separated string of nested scopes, such as `src.utils.data_loader.DataLoader.__init__`.\n    You can set `scope_contains` to a substring of the scope to filter dependencies related to that scope.\n    For example, setting it to `DataLoader` would match the above scope and include its dependencies.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `path_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `scope_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `edge_type`: Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`. If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by edge type.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched edge.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice. It defaults to 12, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time.\n    You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched edges.\n    - `path_limit`: Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include. A dependency path is a sequence of dependency edges connecting two symbols.\n    If there are more matched paths than this limit, only the first few unique compressed paths are returned.\n    Important behavior:\n    - If at least one symbol matches the combined `symbol_contains` / `scope_contains` / `path_contains` filters, only edges whose source or destination is in that matched symbol set are retained.\n    - If no symbol matches those filters, edge filtering falls back to `edge_type` plus pagination only; it does not return an empty edge list.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `dep_summary`: Summary counts, including retained file / symbol / edge counts and the returned / filtered edge counts.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `matched_symbols`: A compact JSON array of symbols related to the current page of results.\n    - `edges`: A JSON array of matched dependency edges.\n    - `paths`: A JSON array of compressed dependency-path strings related to the current page.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched edges are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each symbol in `matched_symbols` has these fields:\n    - `kind`: The raw symbol kind from DaLIC, such as `func`, `class`, or similar labels.\n    - `symbol`: The symbol name.\n    - `scope`: The raw scope string associated with the symbol.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the symbol is defined.\n    - `line`: The line number where the symbol is defined.\n    Each edge in `edges` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `src`: A compact source-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `dst`: A compact target-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `edge_type`: The raw dependency edge type, such as `arg`, `data`, or `ret`.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n    - During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: get_trace_artifacts ----\nDescription: \nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nParameters:\n  (1) artifact_type (string, optional): Optional exact filter for artifact definition type.\nAllowed values: [`all`, `function`, `method`, `class`]\n  (2) path_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.\n  (3) name_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.\n  (4) start (integer, optional): Zero-based pagination offset.\n  (5) limit (integer, optional): Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'pandas-dev' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described workflow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described workflow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Understand the Purpose of the Feature in the Context of the Codebase\n    - Understand what the feature is supposed to do.\n    - Understand why it is needed.\n    - Understand how it fits into the overall functionality of the codebase.\n- Analyze the Feature's Relationship with the Codebase\n    - Identify the key affected components of the feature.\n    - Understand how these components interact with each other and with existing code.\n    - Consider the inputs, outputs, and side effects of the feature.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: DOC: Add examples for float_format in to_csv documentation\n\n### Pandas version checks\r\n\r\n- [X] I have checked that the issue still exists on the latest versions of the docs on `main` [here](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/dev/)\r\n\r\n\r\n### Location of the documentation\r\n\r\nhttps://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.to_csv.html\r\n\r\n### Documentation problem\r\n\r\nThe float_format parameter in to_csv is explained but lacks examples. Users might struggle to understand how to apply this parameter effectively without concrete examples in the documentation.\r\n\r\n### Suggested fix for documentation\r\n\r\nI suggest adding examples for float_format to make the documentation more beginner-friendly. Examples could include:\r\n\r\n```\r\n# Format floats to two decimal places\r\ndf.to_csv(\"example1.csv\", float_format=\"%.2f\")\r\n\r\n# Use scientific notation\r\ndf.to_csv(\"example2.csv\", float_format=\"{:.2e}\".format)\r\n```\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n\n\nSupplemental evidence from software dynamic analysis is available through a tool for this instance: `get_trace_artifacts`. \nThis tool provides compact raw slices of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts, more specifically:\n- `get_trace_artifacts`: The unique execution trace artifacts are obtained through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis. \n    This analysis involves executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled, \n    tracing their e